/** Individually written reports. The figures refer to each specific stimulus;
 * the reports are illustrative responses disclosed only after submission. */
export const academicNumericModels: Record<string, string> = {
  'cycling-safety': `The graph shows annual bicycle trips in a city from 2010 to 2025, while the table records cyclist injury rates in the same years. Trips increased throughout the period, whereas both minor and serious injuries became less frequent per million trips. Minor-injury rates remained considerably higher than serious-injury rates.

Annual cycling rose from 9.2 million trips in 2010 to 12.8 million in 2015 and 17.6 million in 2020. It reached 24.1 million in 2025, an overall gain of 14.9 million. The largest five-year increase was the final one, when trips rose by 6.5 million.

Minor injuries fell from 142 per million trips in 2010 to 128 in 2015, 101 in 2020 and 84 in 2025. The total reduction was therefore 58 injuries per million trips. Serious injuries declined more evenly, from eighteen to fifteen, twelve and finally nine.

The serious-injury rate consequently halved, while the minor-injury rate fell by a smaller proportion. These rates moved downward as the number of trips expanded, so the two visuals describe opposite trends in cycling volume and reported injuries relative to that volume.`,
  'hybrid-working': `The visuals show average weekly home-working days for surveyed employees from 2016 to 2025 and compare four occupations in the final year. Home-working increased overall but finished below its 2021 peak. Software specialists had the highest occupational average in 2025, whereas technical-support staff had the lowest.

The overall figure began at 0.4 days per working week in 2016 and rose modestly to 0.7 in 2019. It then climbed sharply to 2.8 days in 2021, the highest observation. The average dropped to 2.1 days in 2023 before recovering slightly to 2.3 in 2025, still 1.9 days above the starting level.

In the occupational comparison, software specialists averaged 3.4 remote days per week. Administrative staff recorded 2.7 days, placing them seven tenths of a day below software specialists but above the final overall average.

Sales staff worked from home for 1.9 days and technical-support employees for 1.2. The occupational spread was therefore 2.2 days between software and technical support. The four group averages show substantial variation around the overall measure rather than a single pattern applying equally to every type of employee.`,
  'visitor-stays': `The bar chart compares international visitors' average stays by main trip purpose in 2025, while the pie chart shows accommodation among all surveyed visitors. Study visits were much longer than other trips, and business visits shortest. Hotels were the most common accommodation, followed by staying with friends or relatives.

Visitors travelling to study stayed an average of 24.5 nights. Those visiting relatives remained for 13.1 nights, just over half the study figure. Holiday stays averaged 8.6 nights, while business travellers spent only 4.2 nights, making the longest and shortest purposes 20.3 nights apart.

Hotels accommodated 44% of surveyed visitors, seventeen percentage points more than friends or relatives, at 27%. Together these two categories represented 71% of accommodation choices. Rented apartments were the third-largest option, with an 18% share.

Student accommodation accounted for 7%, and other arrangements for the remaining 4%. The accommodation distribution is separate from the trip-purpose comparison: it shows the choices of all visitors rather than identifying where each purpose group stayed. The clearest contrasts are thus the long study stays and the overall dominance of hotels.`,
  'tea-market': `The graph shows national retail tea sales between 2005 and 2025, and the bar chart compares weekly household purchases by the main shopper's age in 2025. Annual sales rose until 2015 and then fell below their starting level. Final-year purchases were higher in households whose main shopper was older.

National tea sales increased from 74 thousand tonnes in 2005 to 78 thousand in 2010 and a peak of 81 thousand in 2015. They subsequently fell to 77 thousand in 2020 and 69 thousand in 2025. The final total was twelve thousand tonnes below the peak and five thousand below the opening figure.

Households with a shopper under thirty bought 82 grams of tea per week on average. Those in the 30–44 group purchased 126 grams, while the 45–59 group bought 174 grams. The oldest group, aged sixty or over, had the highest weekly purchases at 213 grams.

The oldest households therefore bought 131 grams more each week than the youngest, well over twice as much. Purchases increased at each successive age group, contrasting with the decline in overall national sales during the later part of the time series.`,
  'park-visits': `The graph shows annual visits to a city park between 2012 and 2025, while the pie chart divides its maintenance spending in the final year. Visits increased at every observation and almost doubled overall. Grounds maintenance and tree care together accounted for most of the maintenance budget.

The park received 380 thousand visits in 2012. This increased to 425 thousand in 2015, 510 thousand in 2018 and 605 thousand in 2021. By 2025, annual attendance reached 730 thousand, a total increase of 350 thousand. The final interval added 125 thousand visits, more than any of the preceding intervals shown.

Grounds maintenance took the largest budget share, at 37%. Tree care ranked second with 24%, placing their combined proportion at 61%. Paths received 18%, six points less than tree care and less than half the grounds-maintenance share.

Play equipment accounted for 13%, while lighting had the smallest allocation at 8%. The three smaller categories together received 39% of maintenance spending. The visuals therefore show both a sustained expansion in park use and a budget concentrated chiefly on its grounds and trees.`,
  'clinic-access': `The chart compares average one-way travel time to four rural clinics in 2025, and the table shows their weekly hours and number of doctors. Plain clinic combined the shortest journey with the greatest service availability. Ridge had the longest journey, shortest opening week and fewest doctors.

Travel to Plain averaged eighteen minutes, compared with 24 minutes to Valley and 31 to Lake. Ridge required 43 minutes, 25 more than Plain and twelve more than Lake. The difference between the two extremes was therefore substantially larger than the gaps between the other clinics.

Plain opened 48 hours weekly and employed four doctors. Valley offered 42 hours with three doctors, while Lake provided 38 hours with the same number of doctors. Although Valley and Lake had identical staffing totals, Valley was open four hours longer and involved a seven-minute shorter journey.

Ridge operated for thirty hours each week with only two doctors. Its opening time was eighteen hours below Plain's, and its medical staff numbered half as many. Across these measures, the ranking for travel time ran opposite to that for opening hours, with the shortest journey leading to the longest-open clinic.`,
  'online-course': `The graph shows enrolment in a public online-course programme from 2014 to 2025, while the bar chart compares completion rates by subject in the final year. Enrolments increased throughout, more than fivefold overall. Creative arts achieved the highest completion percentage and computing the lowest.

The programme began with eighteen thousand annual enrolments in 2014. This rose to 35 thousand in 2017 and 72 thousand in 2020. The 37-thousand increase between those years was the largest shown. Enrolment then reached 91 thousand in 2023 and 104 thousand in 2025, giving an overall rise of 86 thousand.

In 2025, 67% of creative-arts enrolments were completed. Languages ranked second at 58%, nine percentage points below creative arts. Business recorded 51%, while computing's completion rate was only 46%.

The subject spread was therefore 21 percentage points between the highest and lowest rates. Languages exceeded business by seven points, and business exceeded computing by five. The two visuals highlight growing participation in the programme alongside differing final-year completion outcomes, without identifying the number enrolled in each subject.`,
  'port-fuels': `The graph records average sulphur dioxide concentration at a port monitoring station from 2010 to 2025. The pie chart shows the fuels supplying vessel energy in the final year. Concentration declined throughout, while marine diesel and low-sulphur fuel oil together supplied most vessel energy in 2025.

Sulphur dioxide averaged 39 micrograms per cubic metre in 2010 and fell to 35 in 2013. It dropped further to 29 in 2016 and 24 in 2019, before reaching eighteen in 2022 and fifteen in 2025. The overall reduction was 24 micrograms per cubic metre, with the final level well below half the initial reading.

Marine diesel supplied 48% of vessel energy in 2025, making it the largest source. Low-sulphur fuel oil provided 28%, and liquefied natural gas 15%. These three fuels together accounted for 91% of the total.

Electricity contributed 6%, twice the 3% from other sources. Diesel's share alone was twenty points above fuel oil and more than three times the gas share. The final-year fuel distribution was consequently concentrated in two sources, while the concentration time series showed a continuous decline over the longer period.`,
  'refill-stores': `The graph shows the number of refill shops operating nationally between 2013 and 2025. The bar chart compares average monthly sales per shop for three liquid products in 2025. Shop numbers expanded strongly, while laundry liquid sold considerably more than either dishwashing liquid or hand soap.

There were 24 refill shops in 2013 and 63 in 2016. The total then rose to 118 in 2019, 191 in 2022 and 267 in 2025. Each three-year increase was larger than the preceding one: 39 shops, then 55, 73 and finally 76. The overall gain was 243 shops, bringing the final count to more than eleven times its initial level.

Laundry liquid sold an average of 486 litres per shop each month in 2025. Dishwashing liquid was second, at 312 litres, while hand soap recorded 224 litres. Laundry sales therefore exceeded dishwashing sales by 174 litres and hand-soap sales by 262.

Dishwashing liquid's advantage over hand soap was smaller, at 88 litres. The product figures show laundry liquid selling more than twice as much as hand soap, alongside the broader increase in the number of shops offering refill services.`,
  'fishing-industry': `The graph shows fish landed annually at a regional port from 2000 to 2025, and the table compares fishing and processing jobs at the start and end. Landings declined overall despite a temporary recovery. Fishing employment fell, while processing employment increased and overtook fishing.

The port handled 62 thousand tonnes in 2000. Landings decreased to 58 thousand in 2005, 49 thousand in 2010 and 46 thousand in 2015. They recovered to 51 thousand tonnes in 2020 before dropping to 47 thousand in 2025, fifteen thousand below the starting figure but slightly above the 2015 minimum.

Fishing employed 840 people in 2000, compared with 620 in fish processing. By 2025, fishing jobs had fallen to 510, a loss of 330. Processing jobs rose by 170 to 790, making processing the larger employer.

The initial 220-job advantage for fishing became a 280-job advantage for processing. Combined employment nevertheless declined from 1,460 to 1,300 jobs, because the processing increase did not offset the fishing reduction. Thus, the period saw lower landings and fewer jobs overall, together with a substantial shift in employment towards processing.`,
  'language-summer': `The table compares four residential language courses in summer 2025 by duration, weekly teaching hours and total price. The university course was longest and cost most overall. The mountain course was shortest but provided the most teaching per week, while the coastal option had the lightest weekly timetable.

The university centre offered five weeks of study, with 22 teaching hours each week, for 2,630 currency units. This amounted to 110 teaching hours in total. City college ran for three weeks at 24 hours per week and charged 1,860, giving a total of 72 teaching hours.

The coastal institute also provided 72 hours altogether, but spread them over four weeks at eighteen hours per week. Its price was 1,720, making it 140 units cheaper than City college despite the longer residential period.

Mountain academy offered the most concentrated programme: two weeks with thirty teaching hours per week. Its sixty total hours were the fewest among the courses, and its fee of 1,540 was the lowest. Thus, the lowest total price and shortest stay coincided with the highest weekly teaching intensity, rather than the longest overall amount of instruction.`,
  'ambulance-regions': `The table compares ambulance provision in four districts in 2025, showing population, fleet size and median response time. The inner city had the largest population and fleet but the quickest response. Rural north had the smallest population and fleet, alongside the longest response time.

The inner city served 180 thousand residents with fourteen ambulances, and its median response was eight minutes. The outer suburb had 145 thousand residents and nine vehicles. Its response time was eleven minutes, three minutes longer than in the inner city.

The coastal district occupied an intermediate position on all three measures. Its population was 94 thousand, its fleet consisted of seven ambulances, and the median response was fourteen minutes. It consequently had fewer residents and vehicles than the outer suburb but a longer response.

Rural north served only 62 thousand residents with five ambulances. Nevertheless, its response time reached nineteen minutes, eleven above the inner-city figure and five above the coastal district's. Across the four districts, population and fleet rankings were identical, while the order for speed of response ran in the opposite direction.`,
  'market-stalls': `The table compares stalls, weekly opening hours and annual visits at four food markets in 2025. Station market was largest in stall numbers and visitor totals, whereas Harbour traded for the longest time. Village market had the lowest figure on every indicator.

Station market contained 84 stalls and received 360 thousand annual visits. It opened for fourteen hours a week. Harbour had fewer stalls, at 56, and fewer visits, at 285 thousand, despite operating for eighteen hours weekly. Station therefore attracted 75 thousand more visits while trading for four fewer hours.

College market was smaller than either of these, with 47 stalls and 174 thousand annual visits. Its twelve weekly opening hours also placed it third. Village market had only 32 stalls, opened for eight hours and attracted 92 thousand visits per year.

The contrast between the extremes was substantial: Station had 52 more stalls and 268 thousand more visits than Village. Opening-time differences were less directly aligned with the other indicators, since Harbour led on hours but not on stalls or visits. The table therefore shows no single market leading across all three measures.`,
  'recycling-centres': `The table compares four household recycling centres in 2025 by the range of materials accepted, weekly opening hours and annual waste received. East depot ranked first on every measure and South depot last. West consistently recorded higher figures than North, placing it second throughout.

East depot accepted 23 material types and opened sixty hours a week. It received 18,750 tonnes of waste annually. West offered 21 material categories, operated for 56 weekly hours and handled 15,900 tonnes, making it the closest centre to East on each indicator.

North depot accepted eighteen types of material, with 48 opening hours and 12,400 tonnes received annually. South was lower again, at sixteen material types, 42 hours and 9,600 tonnes. North therefore received 2,800 tonnes more waste than South, while opening six additional hours per week.

Comparing the highest and lowest centres, East accepted seven more material types and provided eighteen extra opening hours. Its annual intake exceeded South's by 9,150 tonnes, nearly matching South's entire intake. The ordering was consequently consistent across service range, availability and the amount of waste delivered to each site.`,
  'walking-trails': `The table compares four marked walking routes in a national park by length, ascent and estimated completion time. The Ridge path was the longest and involved the greatest climb, with the highest time estimate. The River loop was shortest and had the least ascent and lowest estimated time.

The Ridge path covered fourteen kilometres and climbed 620 metres. Walkers were advised to allow five hours. In comparison, the Forest circuit was eleven kilometres long, involved 240 metres of ascent and had an estimated duration of three hours. The ridge route therefore added three kilometres but 380 metres of climbing.

The Lake route measured nine kilometres, with a 130-metre ascent and a two-and-a-half-hour estimate. It was two kilometres shorter than the forest circuit and involved 110 metres less climbing, while reducing the suggested walking time by half an hour.

The River loop was only six kilometres long, climbed 85 metres and took an estimated one and a half hours. Across the routes, all three indicators followed the same order. The largest contrast was between the ridge and river routes, separated by eight kilometres, 535 metres of ascent and three and a half hours.`,
  'childrens-books': `The table compares children's reading programmes at four library branches in 2025. Hill branch provided the most sessions and had the most registered children, but the lowest average attendance rate. Canal branch showed the reverse pattern, with the smallest programme and highest proportional attendance.

Hill offered nine sessions each week for 164 registered children, recording a 76% attendance rate. Park was second for programme size, with seven sessions and 126 children. Its attendance rate was higher at 81%, despite the smaller number of weekly activities.

Civic provided six sessions for 118 registered children and achieved 84% attendance. Canal had only five weekly sessions and 93 registrations, but its attendance rate reached 88%. The four branches were therefore ordered differently by programme size and attendance percentage.

Hill registered 71 more children than Canal and ran four extra sessions per week, yet its attendance rate was twelve percentage points lower. Park and Civic were closer: Park had eight more children and one more weekly session, but Civic's attendance rate exceeded Park's by three points. Larger registration totals consequently did not coincide with stronger proportional attendance in this comparison.`,
  'water-tests': `The table compares drinking-water sampling programmes in four regions during 2025. Metro conducted the most tests from the most locations, while River had the highest percentage meeting the standard. Upland had the fewest sampling sites and annual samples, as well as the lowest compliance rate.

Metro took 576 samples from 48 locations, with 98.4% meeting the required standard. Coastal sampled 39 locations and collected 468 samples, of which 97.2% met the standard. Both regions therefore averaged twelve samples per site over the year.

River also averaged twelve samples per location, collecting 420 samples from 35 sites. Its compliance rate of 99.1% was 0.7 percentage points above Metro and 1.9 above Coastal, despite its smaller testing programme.

Upland took 248 samples from 31 locations, equivalent to eight per site. Its compliance rate was 96.8%, 2.3 points below River's. The largest programme thus did not have the highest compliance percentage. The table's rankings differed according to whether the comparison concerned sampling volume, site coverage or the proportion of tested samples that satisfied the drinking-water standard.`,
  'graduate-pathways': `The table shows the destinations of graduates from four university disciplines six months after graduation in 2025. Paid employment was the largest category in every discipline. Business had the highest employment share, while biological sciences had the largest proportion continuing to study.

Business graduates recorded 78% in paid employment, followed by engineering at 73%. Their further-study figures were relatively low, at 12% and 19% respectively. Other destinations accounted for 10% in business and 8% in engineering, the latter being the smallest other-destination share in the table.

Among humanities graduates, 61% were employed and 22% were in further study. The remaining 17% had another destination, the highest such proportion among the disciplines. Biological sciences had the lowest employment share, at 49%, but a much larger further-study figure of 38%. Its other-destination share was 13%.

The employment gap between business and biological sciences was 29 percentage points. Their further-study gap ran in the opposite direction, at 26 points. Biological sciences consequently showed the most balanced distribution between employment and additional education, whereas paid work clearly dominated the other three disciplines.`,
  'museum-energy': `The table compares floor area, annual electricity consumption and solar share at four museums in 2025. The Science museum was the largest and consumed the most electricity, while the local-history museum was smallest and consumed least. Solar proportions followed a different ranking, with local history highest and the City museum lowest.

The Science museum covered 7,800 square metres and used 1,240 MWh of electricity annually, 18% of which was supplied by solar energy. The City museum had 6,400 square metres and consumed 910 MWh, but its solar share was only 12%.

The Maritime museum was smaller again, at 4,200 square metres, with annual electricity use of 580 MWh. Nevertheless, its 26% solar share was fourteen percentage points above the City museum's and eight above the Science museum's.

Local history occupied 2,900 square metres and used 365 MWh per year. Solar supplied 31%, five points more than at Maritime. The difference between the largest and smallest buildings was 4,900 square metres and 875 MWh of annual electricity, but the smaller museum led by thirteen points in solar share.`,
  'nursery-plants': `The table compares sapling output, native-species range and one-year survival at four tree nurseries in 2024. Coastal grove produced the most saplings but had the fewest native species and lowest survival rate. Northern ridge produced least, yet led on both species variety and survival.

Coastal grove supplied 216 thousand saplings, offered eleven native species and recorded 87% survival. Green valley was second in output, with 185 thousand saplings. It offered fourteen native species, and its 91% survival rate exceeded Coastal's by four percentage points.

River nursery produced 169 thousand saplings and had sixteen native species. Its survival rate was 93%, putting it second on both of these latter indicators. Northern ridge's output was only 142 thousand, but it offered eighteen native species and achieved 94% survival.

The two extreme nurseries illustrate the contrasting rankings particularly clearly. Coastal produced 74 thousand more saplings than Northern ridge, while offering seven fewer native species and recording a seven-point lower survival rate. Green valley and River occupied intermediate positions, with River's smaller production accompanied by a broader native range and higher survival percentage.`,
  'adult-exams': `The table compares four professional qualification courses in 2025, showing enrolment, duration and completion. Accounting attracted the most learners, while building inspection had the fewest. Health support was the longest course and achieved the highest completion rate; network administration had the lowest completion percentage.

The accounting certificate enrolled 320 people, ran for nine months and recorded 83% completion. Network administration enrolled 286 learners over eight months, but only 79% completed. It was therefore slightly shorter and smaller than accounting, with a four-point lower completion rate.

The health-support diploma had 245 learners and lasted twelve months, the longest duration in the table. Its completion rate reached 91%, eight points above accounting and twelve above network administration. Building inspection had only 178 learners and took six months, yet its 88% completion rate was second highest.

Duration and enrolment consequently had no single shared relationship with completion. The shortest course completed at a higher rate than two longer programmes, while the longest programme performed best overall. Enrolment varied by 142 learners between accounting and building inspection, compared with a twelve-point spread across completion percentages.`,
  'textile-disposal': `The charts compare the disposal of unwanted household clothing by weight in a city in 2009 and 2025. General rubbish remained the largest single destination but became much less dominant. Donation, recycling and resale all increased their proportions, while the other category was unchanged.

In 2009, 58% of clothing went into general rubbish, more than twice the 24% donated to charity. Textile recycling accounted for 9%, resale for 5% and other routes for the remaining 4%. The rubbish share was therefore greater than all alternatives combined.

By 2025, general rubbish had fallen to 31%, only two percentage points above charity donation at 29%. Recycling rose to 22%, more than double its earlier share, and resale increased to 14%. Other disposal methods remained at 4%.

The 27-point reduction in rubbish was matched by gains across the three named alternatives: five points for donation, thirteen for recycling and nine for resale. Together, donation and resale rose from 29% to 43% of discarded clothing weight. The final distribution was consequently much more balanced than the original one, in which rubbish had clearly dominated.`,
  'research-funding': `The charts compare the sources of a research institute's income in 2010 and 2025, when total funding rose from twelve to eighteen million currency units. Government grants remained the largest source but lost share. Industry and international funding became more prominent, and all sources increased in monetary value.

Government grants fell from 52% of income to 39%, but their value grew from 6.24 to 7.02 million because the total budget expanded. Industry contracts increased from 21% to 28%, doubling in amount from 2.52 to 5.04 million and remaining the second-largest source.

International grants showed the strongest proportional change, rising from 8% to 16%. Their amount tripled from 0.96 to 2.88 million, putting them ahead of charitable grants in 2025. Charitable funding's share declined slightly, from 14% to 12%, while its value rose from 1.68 to 2.16 million.

Other income retained a 5% share, equivalent to 0.6 million initially and 0.9 million later. Thus, declining shares for government and charity funding did not mean financial reductions; the larger overall income increased the amount available from both.`,
  'reserve-habitats': `The charts show land-cover shares in a wildlife reserve in 1990 and 2025. The reserve remained 2,000 hectares in area. Woodland was the largest category in both years, while wetland expanded markedly and farmland became much less extensive.

In 1990, woodland occupied 44% of the reserve, equivalent to 880 hectares. Grassland was the second-largest category at 28%, followed by wetland at 12% and farmland at 11%. Other land cover accounted for 5%.

By 2025, woodland had increased modestly to 47%, or 940 hectares. Wetland rose to 22%, overtaking grassland, whose share declined to 21%. In area terms, wetland grew from 240 to 440 hectares, whereas grassland fell from 560 to 420 hectares.

Farmland showed the largest relative contraction, dropping from 11% to 4%, or from 220 to eighty hectares. Other land cover increased slightly to 6%, equal to 120 hectares. Woodland and wetland together therefore covered 69% of the reserve at the end, compared with 56% initially, while grassland and farmland together declined from 39% to 25%.`,
  'festival-income': `The charts compare income sources for an arts festival in 2011 and 2025. Total revenue increased from 800,000 to 1.2 million currency units. Ticket sales remained the main source but lost share, while corporate sponsorship overtook public subsidy as the second-largest contribution.

Tickets generated 61% of income in 2011, followed by public subsidy at 18% and sponsorship at 12%. Food stalls contributed 6%, while merchandise provided only 3%. Ticket sales therefore supplied substantially more than all other sources combined.

By 2025, the ticket share had fallen to 48%, although ticket revenue rose from 488,000 to 576,000 currency units. Sponsorship increased to 23%, with its value growing from 96,000 to 276,000. Public subsidy declined to 14% of the total but still rose in amount, from 144,000 to 168,000.

The smaller commercial sources also expanded. Food-stall income reached 10% and merchandise 5%, each considerably above its earlier share. Together they supplied 15% of the final budget, against 9% initially. The festival's income consequently became less dependent on tickets even though the money earned from ticket sales increased.`,
  'freshwater-use': `The charts show how freshwater withdrawals in a region were divided among five purposes in 2000 and 2025. Irrigation remained the largest category but lost share. Household use grew enough to replace industry as the second-largest purpose, while the other categories increased more modestly.

In 2000, irrigation accounted for 54% of withdrawals, more than half the total. Industry took 23%, households 15% and power generation 6%. The remaining 2% served other purposes. Irrigation and industry together represented 77% of all water withdrawn.

By 2025, irrigation's share had fallen eleven percentage points to 43%. Industry also declined, from 23% to 19%. In contrast, households rose to 26%, an eleven-point increase that put them seven points ahead of industry.

Power generation increased from 6% to 9%, while other uses rose from 2% to 3%. The combined household and power-generation share consequently grew from 21% to 35%. Irrigation and industry still formed the majority at 62%, but their dominance was weaker than at the start. These distributions concern withdrawn water, rather than the volume finally consumed by each sector.`,
  'housing-tenure': `The charts compare housing tenure among households formed within the preceding year in 2005 and 2025. Ownership with a mortgage was initially the most common arrangement, but private rental became the largest category. Both types of ownership declined in share, while public rental changed relatively little.

In 2005, 42% of new households owned with a mortgage and 11% owned without one, giving a combined ownership share of 53%. Private rental accounted for 29%, public rental for 14% and other arrangements for 4%. Ownership with a mortgage exceeded private rental by thirteen percentage points.

Twenty years later, private rental had risen to 43%, fourteen points above its earlier figure. Mortgaged ownership fell to 31%, while ownership without a mortgage declined to 8%. Their combined share was consequently only 39%, below private renting alone.

Public rental dropped by two points to 12%, and other arrangements increased by two to 6%. Private rental's final advantage over mortgaged ownership was twelve percentage points, reversing the initial ranking. The clearest shift was therefore between these two large categories, rather than a substantial change in public rental.`,
  'hospital-energy': `The charts compare a regional hospital's energy sources in 2008 and 2025, measured as shares of energy consumption. Grid electricity remained the largest source, but solar became much more important. Natural gas and oil both lost share, with oil showing a particularly sharp decline.

In 2008, grid electricity supplied 47% of the hospital's energy and natural gas 38%. Together these sources accounted for 85%. Oil contributed 11%, while solar and other sources were minor components, each providing 2%.

By 2025, grid electricity had fallen to 42% and natural gas to 29%. Despite these reductions, they remained the two largest sources and together still provided 71% of energy. Oil declined by eight percentage points to only 3%, becoming the smallest category.

Solar rose nineteen points to 21%, moving comfortably into third place behind gas. Other sources increased to 5%, also overtaking oil. The combined solar-and-other share reached 26%, compared with just 4% in 2008. This contrasts with the gas-and-oil total, which fell from 49% to 32%, showing a substantial redistribution within the hospital's energy mix.`,
  'radio-airtime': `The charts compare the distribution of a radio station's weekly airtime among five programme types in 1995 and 2025. Music remained the largest category but occupied a smaller share, while talk shows expanded enough to overtake news. Documentaries also gained airtime proportionally, whereas sport declined slightly.

In 1995, music accounted for 48% of broadcasting, more than twice the 22% allocated to news. Talk shows received 12%, sport 10% and documentaries 8%. Music and news together therefore occupied 70% of the station's schedule.

By 2025, music had fallen fourteen percentage points to 34%. News declined more moderately to 19%, leaving their combined share at 53%. Talk shows increased fifteen points to 27%, more than doubling their earlier proportion and coming within seven points of music.

Documentaries rose from 8% to 12%, overtaking sport, whose share fell to 8%. The two categories had consequently reversed their initial order. Overall, talk shows and documentaries together grew from one fifth of airtime to 39%, while the previously dominant combination of music and news became considerably less prominent.`,
  'fruit-sales': `The charts show the fruit sales of a growers cooperative by weight in 2010 and 2025. Total sales increased from 5,000 to 8,000 tonnes. Apples retained the largest share, while berries expanded strongly and replaced pears as the second-largest category. Every fruit increased in tonnage despite several falling shares.

Apples fell from 41% to 33% of sales, but their quantity rose from 2,050 to 2,640 tonnes. Pears declined from 26% to 19%, while increasing from 1,300 to 1,520 tonnes. Peaches changed only slightly in share, from 17% to 16%, equivalent to growth from 850 to 1,280 tonnes.

Berries showed the most striking development. Their share climbed from 6% to 20%, and their weight rose from 300 to 1,600 tonnes. They moved ahead of both peaches and pears, although still remaining below apples.

Plums increased from 10% to 12%, with sales rising from 500 to 960 tonnes. The cooperative's distribution thus became less concentrated in apples and pears, whose combined share fell from 67% to 52%. Berries contributed 1,300 of the additional 3,000 tonnes sold overall.`,
  'coding-lab': `The charts compare how booked computer hours at a public digital learning centre were distributed among activities in 2014 and 2025. Basic computer skills remained the largest category, but coding nearly matched it by the end. Coding and digital design both gained substantial shares, while job applications became slightly less prominent.

In 2014, basic skills accounted for 45% of booked hours, considerably more than job applications at 24%. Coding occupied 12%, digital design 10% and other activities 9%. Basic skills and job applications together represented more than two thirds of computer use.

By 2025, the basic-skills share had fallen nineteen percentage points to 26%, only one point above coding at 25%. Coding had therefore more than doubled its proportion. Digital design rose nine points to 19%, leaving it close to job applications, which declined to 21%.

Other activities remained at 9%. Coding and design together accounted for 44% of booked hours in the later year, exactly twice their combined 2014 share. Meanwhile, basic skills and job applications fell from 69% to 47%, indicating a more balanced distribution of computer time among the main activities.`,
  'car-service': `The charts compare the distribution of a vehicle workshop's revenue among five services in 2006 and 2025. Routine servicing remained the largest income source but lost share. Electrical work grew markedly and overtook engine repairs, while tyres and bodywork increased more modestly.

In 2006, routine servicing supplied 46% of revenue, followed by engine repairs at 27%. Tyres accounted for 14%, bodywork 9% and electrical work only 4%. The two leading services together generated 73% of the workshop's income, making the remaining categories relatively minor contributors.

By 2025, routine servicing had fallen to 35% and engine repairs to 18%. Their combined share was therefore 53%, twenty percentage points below its earlier level. Electrical work rose to 20%, five times its initial share, placing it second and two points above engine repairs.

Tyres increased three percentage points to 17%, coming close to the engine-repair figure. Bodywork edged up to 10% but became the smallest revenue category because electrical work had expanded so much. The final distribution was thus less dominated by the original two leaders and more evenly spread among servicing, electrical work, engine repairs and tyres.`,
  'roof-heat': `The chart compares the surface temperatures of four roofing materials at noon and at 4 p.m. on a summer day. Dark tiles were substantially hotter than the other surfaces at both readings, while the vegetated roof was coolest. Every material cooled between the two observations.

At noon, dark tiles reached 61 degrees Celsius. Light tiles were fifteen degrees cooler, at 46 degrees, while reflective coating measured 38 degrees. The vegetated roof recorded only 34 degrees, producing a 27-degree difference from the dark-tile surface.

By 4 p.m., dark tiles had fallen to 54 degrees, the largest reduction among the four materials. Light tiles declined by four degrees to 42, and reflective coating dropped by three to 35. The vegetated roof changed least, cooling by two degrees to 32.

Although the ranking remained identical, the contrasts became slightly smaller later in the day. Dark tiles were twelve degrees above light tiles at 4 p.m., compared with fifteen at noon. Their lead over the vegetated roof narrowed from 27 to 22 degrees, but dark tiles still retained a considerably higher surface temperature than every alternative shown.`,
  'preventive-dental': `The chart shows the proportion of adults attending a preventive dental appointment in four age groups in 2013 and 2025. Attendance increased in every group. People aged 30–44 had the highest rate in both years, while those aged sixty and above had the lowest, despite making the largest improvement.

In 2013, 61% of adults aged 30–44 attended, followed by 55% of the 45–59 group. The younger and older extremes were less likely to attend, with rates of 42% among 18–29-year-olds and 38% among those aged sixty or over.

By 2025, the rate for 30–44-year-olds had risen to 69%, an eight-point increase. Attendance among people aged 45–59 reached 66%, reducing the gap behind the leading group from six percentage points to three.

The two initially lower groups showed larger gains. The youngest group increased by fifteen points to 57%, while the oldest rose by sixteen to 54%. Consequently, the difference between the highest and lowest age-group rates narrowed from 23 percentage points in 2013 to fifteen in 2025, although the overall order remained unchanged.`,
  'public-hearings': `The chart compares participation in local planning consultation among residents of four housing types in 2025. Submitting written comments was more common than attending a hearing in every category. Owners of flats participated most on both measures, whereas hearing attendance was lowest among residents of rented flats.

Among flat owners, 38% submitted comments and 22% attended a hearing. The corresponding figures for owners of houses were 31% and 19%. Owners therefore had higher participation than renters within each of the two broad property types.

Residents of rented houses recorded a 24% written-comment rate and a 13% hearing-attendance rate. Those in rented flats were somewhat more likely to write, at 27%, but less likely to attend, at 11%. This made rented flats the only housing type whose written and in-person rankings differed markedly.

The gap between the two methods was sixteen percentage points for both categories of flats. It was smaller for houses, at twelve points for owners and eleven for renters. Written comments thus attracted a wider proportion of residents, but the two measures should be treated separately because an individual could take part through both channels.`,
  'composting': `The chart compares household participation in composting food scraps and garden waste in 2024, grouped by garden size. Composting became more common as garden size increased. Garden waste was composted more often than food scraps in all households with a garden, while homes without one showed the reverse pattern.

Among households with no garden, 18% composted food scraps but only 4% composted garden waste. Participation was much higher in homes with a small garden, where the figures were 39% and 46% respectively. This was also the narrowest difference between the two waste types among garden-owning households.

Medium-sized gardens were associated with a 51% food-scrap composting rate and a 68% garden-waste rate. Both percentages increased further for large gardens, to 58% and 82%. Garden-waste participation therefore rose more sharply across the categories than food-scrap participation.

Comparing the extremes, the food-scrap rate differed by forty percentage points between homes without gardens and those with large gardens. The equivalent difference for garden waste was 78 points. In the large-garden group, garden-waste composting exceeded food-scrap composting by 24 percentage points.`,
  'flood-awareness': `The chart shows the percentages of respondents in four age groups who correctly recognised a flood-warning symbol and identified their nearest evacuation point in 2025. Symbol recognition was consistently stronger than knowledge of the evacuation location. Respondents aged 40–59 performed best on both questions, while those aged 16–24 had the lowest rates.

In the youngest group, 64% recognised the warning symbol but only 37% could name the evacuation point. The 25–39 group achieved higher figures of 79% and 55%, although a substantial difference between the two kinds of knowledge remained.

The strongest results were recorded among people aged 40–59: 85% identified the symbol and 68% knew the evacuation point. Respondents aged sixty and above had lower rates, at 72% and 61%, but still outperformed both younger groups on evacuation-point knowledge.

The difference between the two answers narrowed with age. It stood at 27 percentage points among the youngest respondents and 24 among those aged 25–39, falling to seventeen in the 40–59 group and eleven among the oldest. Thus, symbol knowledge was more widespread, but the oldest group had the most balanced performance.`,
  'research-methods': `The chart compares the use of interviews and numerical surveys in dissertations from four university departments in 2024. Interviews were more prevalent in education and sociology, whereas surveys were more common in business and environmental studies. Sociology led in interview use, while business had the highest survey percentage.

Interviews appeared in 68% of sociology dissertations and 62% of those in education. Their survey figures were lower, at 56% and 45% respectively. The difference between methods was therefore twelve percentage points in sociology and seventeen in education.

Business showed the strongest preference in the opposite direction. Numerical surveys were used in 71% of dissertations, compared with only 34% using interviews, a 37-point gap. Environmental studies also favoured surveys, with rates of 63% against 39% for interviews.

Across departments, interview use varied by 34 percentage points between sociology and business. Survey use was less dispersed, ranging from 45% in education to 71% in business. The percentages describe use of each method separately: dissertations combining interviews with surveys contribute to both measures, so a department's two figures need not total 100%.`,
  'childcare-hours': `The chart compares mothers' and fathers' average weekly unpaid childcare hours across four employment categories in 2025. Mothers spent more time on childcare in every category. Parents outside paid work reported the greatest number of hours, while full-time employees reported the fewest.

Mothers in full-time employment averaged 24 hours of childcare per week, compared with sixteen for fathers. Among self-employed parents, the respective figures were 29 and 21 hours. The difference between mothers and fathers was therefore eight hours in both of these categories.

Part-time employed mothers spent 35 hours on childcare, twelve more than part-time employed fathers, who averaged 23. This was the widest mother–father difference shown. Both figures were above the corresponding full-time averages, but well below those for parents without paid employment.

Among the latter, mothers reported 48 hours and fathers 39, a nine-hour difference. Comparing employment extremes, mothers outside paid work spent twice as much time on childcare as full-time employed mothers. For fathers, the difference was 23 hours, with those not in paid work spending considerably more than double the full-time figure.`,
  'allergen-checks': `The chart shows how often shoppers in four age groups checked allergen labels on unfamiliar food products in 2025. Always checking was most common among people aged 45–59, while sometimes checking was highest among the youngest shoppers. Only the youngest group checked sometimes more often than always.

Among 18–29-year-olds, 27% always checked and 49% sometimes did so, a 22-point difference. The pattern reversed for shoppers aged 30–44, whose figures were 44% for always and 41% for sometimes. These two rates were much closer than those for any other group.

The 45–59 group recorded the highest always-checking percentage, at 51%, alongside a 33% sometimes-checking rate. For those aged sixty and over, both figures were slightly lower, at 46% and 29%. The difference between the two frequencies was eighteen points in the former group and seventeen in the latter.

Across ages, always checking rose from the youngest group to middle age before falling modestly among older shoppers. Sometimes checking moved in the opposite direction, declining at each successive age group. The chart covers these two response categories, rather than showing every possible checking frequency.`,
  'night-trains': `The chart compares the proportions of overnight trains arriving within ten minutes of schedule on four routes in 2014 and 2025. Punctuality improved on three routes, but declined on the capital service. The coastal route overtook the capital route to become the strongest performer in 2025.

In 2014, the capital route led clearly at 91%. The coastal route was second with 76%, followed by the border route at 72% and the mountain route at 68%. The initial spread between the highest and lowest results was therefore 23 percentage points.

By 2025, the coastal service had reached 89%, thirteen points above its earlier figure. The mountain route made an equally large improvement, rising to 81%, but remained the least punctual of the four. Border services increased by twelve points to 84%.

The capital route alone deteriorated, falling four points to 87%. It consequently ended two points below the coastal route while still outperforming the border and mountain services. The difference between the best and worst routes narrowed to eight percentage points, considerably smaller than in 2014, as the initially weaker services improved.`,
  'volunteer-shifts': `The chart compares monthly volunteer hours devoted to administration and coaching in four types of amateur sports club in 2025. Coaching accounted for more time than administration in every sport. Swimming showed the largest difference between the two activities, while tennis showed the smallest.

Swimming volunteers spent an average of 21 hours per month coaching, the highest figure in the chart, and six hours on administration, the lowest administrative figure. The resulting fifteen-hour difference was substantially greater than for the other sports.

Football volunteers reported 17 coaching hours and 8 administrative hours. Athletics had a slightly lower coaching figure of 15 hours but a higher administrative total of 11, placing it first for administration. These two activities were therefore separated by nine hours in football and only four in athletics.

Tennis recorded twelve hours of coaching and nine of administration, giving a three-hour gap. Coaching time varied by nine hours between swimming and tennis, whereas administrative time varied by just five between swimming and athletics. The overall pattern was thus more dispersed for coaching, even though it remained the larger commitment in each sport.`,
  'printer-lifespan': `The chart compares average service life for new and remanufactured office equipment in four categories. New units lasted somewhat longer in every case. Large copiers had the longest lifespan under both conditions, whereas label printers had the shortest.

New large copiers remained in service for 8.1 years on average, compared with 7.5 years for remanufactured units. Document scanners ranked second, at 7.2 and 6.9 years respectively. The difference between new and remanufactured scanners was only 0.3 years, half the gap shown in the other categories.

Desktop printers recorded lifespans of 6.4 years when new and 5.8 years when remanufactured. Label printers were lower on both measures, lasting 5.7 and 5.1 years. Their 0.6-year difference matched that for desktop printers and large copiers.

Comparing equipment types, a new large copier lasted 2.4 years longer than a new label printer. The same 2.4-year spread applied to the remanufactured versions. The order of the categories was identical regardless of condition, and the relatively small within-category differences contrasted with the larger variation between the longest- and shortest-lived equipment.`,
  'food-parcels': `The graph compares monthly food-parcel deliveries by three community organisations at six points during 2025. Overall, the two larger organisations delivered similar numbers by November, while Student Volunteers increased substantially from a much smaller starting point. Neighbourhood Trust lost its early lead temporarily but recovered it at the end.

In January, Neighbourhood Trust distributed 640 parcels, compared with 480 from Community Pantry and 150 from Student Volunteers. The Trust's total fell to 580 in May before recovering. Meanwhile, Community Pantry increased its deliveries at every observation, reaching 635 in July and thereby moving ahead of the Trust's 605.

Both major providers expanded later in the year. Community Pantry remained slightly ahead in September, at 710 against 690, but the order reversed in November: the Trust delivered 760 parcels and the Pantry 745. Their initial difference of 160 parcels had therefore narrowed to fifteen.

Student Volunteers rose to 210 deliveries in May, then dipped to 190 in July. It subsequently reached 265 in September and 320 in November, more than double its January figure, although still well below either of the larger organisations.`,
  'vet-wait': `The line graph shows average waits for non-emergency farm visits by veterinary services in three districts between 2011 and 2025. Waiting times ended lower in all districts. North district changed from the slowest service to the quickest, whereas East and West first experienced longer waits before improving.

North district's average declined throughout the period, from 12 days in 2011 to 11 in 2014 and 8 in 2017. It then fell to 7 days in 2020, 5 in 2023 and 4 in 2025. This eight-day reduction was the largest overall improvement shown.

East district began at nine days, three days below North. Its wait increased to eleven days in 2017, making it the longest of the three at that point. It subsequently shortened to nine days in 2020 and six by the end, but remained the slowest service in the final comparison.

West initially had the shortest wait, at six days. Its figure climbed gradually to a nine-day peak in 2020 before falling to five in 2025. Thus, the gap between the longest and shortest waits narrowed from six days at the start to only two at the end.`,
  'multilingual-tours': `The graph presents annual bookings for museum tours in English, Spanish and Mandarin from 2010 to 2025, measured in thousands. English was the most popular language throughout. Mandarin grew considerably faster than Spanish and overtook it towards the end of the period.

English tours attracted 48 thousand bookings in 2010, compared with seventeen thousand for Spanish and just five thousand for Mandarin. English bookings increased to 57 thousand in 2016, slipped to 54 thousand in 2019 and then recovered to 68 thousand in 2025. Despite this temporary decline, the English series retained a substantial lead.

Spanish bookings rose steadily, reaching 24 thousand in 2016, 29 thousand in 2019 and 35 thousand at the final observation. Mandarin started far below that level but increased from eight thousand in 2013 to 22 thousand in 2019. By 2022 it had moved ahead, with 33 thousand bookings against 31 thousand for Spanish.

Mandarin finished at 42 thousand, seven thousand above Spanish. Its gap behind English narrowed from 43 thousand bookings initially to 26 thousand, although English still accounted for the largest number among these three tour languages.`,
  'rechargeable-tools': `The graph compares reservations for sewing machines, woodworking benches and electronics stations at a public repair workshop during six months of 2024. Reservations increased overall for all equipment types. Sewing machines replaced woodworking benches as the most frequently booked resource, while electronics stations nearly caught up with the benches by December.

In February, woodworking benches led with 112 reservations, followed by sewing machines at 85 and electronics stations at 42. Bench bookings rose to 126 in April but declined over the next two observations to 110 in August. Sewing-machine reservations reached 128 in June, putting them ahead of benches, before dropping to 117 in August.

Both categories recovered later. Sewing machines climbed to 146 reservations in October and 158 in December, while benches increased to 134 and then 140. Consequently, sewing machines ended eighteen reservations ahead of benches, despite beginning 27 behind.

Electronics stations showed continuous growth, rising to 79 reservations in June, 101 in August and 137 in December. Their final total was more than three times the February figure and only three reservations below that for woodworking benches.`,
  'heat-nights': `The graph compares mean summer night temperatures in three city areas from 2000 to 2025. The central commercial area remained warmest and the wooded fringe coolest. All three areas became warmer overall, with a small decline at the 2015 observation interrupting the upward pattern.

In 2000, the commercial centre averaged 21.4 degrees Celsius, compared with 19.1 in the residential suburb and 17.8 on the wooded fringe. By 2010 these readings had risen to 22.8, 20.2 and 18.7 degrees respectively. The difference between the centre and the fringe had consequently widened from 3.6 to 4.1 degrees.

The 2015 figures were slightly lower than those for 2010: 22.5 degrees in the centre, 20.1 in the suburb and 18.5 at the fringe. All three then rose again, reaching their highest displayed values in 2025.

At the end, the centre recorded 24.1 degrees, the suburb 21.8 and the fringe 20.2. Overall increases were therefore 2.7, 2.7 and 2.4 degrees respectively. The final centre-to-fringe difference was 3.9 degrees, marginally greater than at the start.`,
  'renewable-apprentices': `The graph shows applications for three energy-related apprenticeship programmes between 2014 and 2025. Solar installation had the strongest growth and became the leading programme. Wind maintenance also expanded markedly, whereas building insulation increased comparatively little after an early fall.

Building insulation initially attracted 410 applications, comfortably ahead of solar installation at 280 and wind maintenance at 190. By 2017, however, solar applications had reached 425 while insulation had slipped to 395, reversing the order of the two largest programmes. Wind applications increased more modestly to 240.

Solar remained in first place thereafter, rising to 610 applications in 2020, 745 in 2023 and 890 in 2025. Wind maintenance accelerated over the same period, reaching 385, 590 and 680. It overtook insulation by 2023, when the latter recorded 475 applications.

Insulation finished with 510 applications, only one hundred more than its initial figure. Solar gained 610 overall and wind gained 490. The solar programme's final lead over insulation was 380 applications, a substantial reversal of the 130-application deficit seen at the beginning.`,
  'archive-downloads': `The line graph records downloads from three online historical collections between 2012 and 2024, in thousands. Photographs and oral histories grew throughout the period, while digitised newspapers declined at the end. Newspapers initially led, but photographs became the most downloaded collection in 2024.

At the start, newspapers received 83 thousand downloads, photographs 64 thousand and oral histories twelve thousand. Newspaper downloads climbed to 101 thousand in 2015, 126 thousand in 2018 and 151 thousand in 2021. Their subsequent fall to 144 thousand allowed photographs to move ahead.

Photographic downloads increased to 108 thousand in 2018 and 142 thousand in 2021, before reaching 155 thousand at the final observation. Their final advantage over newspapers was eleven thousand, whereas they had begun nineteen thousand behind.

Oral histories remained the least downloaded collection but showed particularly strong growth. Downloads rose from 39 thousand in 2018 to 76 thousand in 2021 and 118 thousand in 2024. This represented an overall gain of 106 thousand, larger than the increases for photographs or newspapers, while still leaving oral histories 37 thousand below photographs at the end.`,
  'reservoir-levels': `The graph shows the percentage of usable storage filled in three reservoirs from May to October 2025. All three lost water storage over much of the period and then recovered, but none returned to its May percentage. Riverbank replaced Upland as the fullest reservoir on this measure.

Upland began at 88% of usable capacity, compared with 72% at Riverbank and 65% at Southern. It declined particularly sharply, falling to 65% in July and 49% in August. Riverbank was then fuller, at 54%, while Southern stood at 44%, its lowest displayed reading.

Upland continued down to 45% in September before recovering to 57% in October. Riverbank reached its minimum of 51% in September and then rose to 63%, maintaining its final lead. Southern's recovery started earlier, with readings of 48% in September and 56% in October.

The reservoirs were much closer together at the end. Upland's initial 23-point lead over Southern was reduced to one percentage point, and the final spread across all three reservoirs was seven points. Upland nevertheless experienced the largest overall decline, at 31 percentage points.`,
  'adult-orchestras': `The graph compares membership of a community choir, an amateur orchestra and a folk ensemble between 2007 and 2025. All three groups expanded overall. The choir remained the largest and the folk ensemble the smallest, although the latter more than tripled in size.

The choir began with 145 members, against 98 in the orchestra and 32 in the folk ensemble. Choir membership rose to 172 in 2013, dipped slightly to 169 in 2016, and then increased at every remaining observation, reaching 218 in 2025.

The orchestra followed a generally upward but less consistent path. Its membership reached 128 in 2016 before falling to 121 in 2019. Recovery took it to 137 in 2022 and 146 in 2025, an overall gain of 48 members.

The folk ensemble grew continuously, from 41 members in 2010 to seventy in 2016, 94 in 2022 and 103 in the final year. It added 71 members altogether, almost matching the choir's absolute gain of 73 despite its much smaller starting size. Nevertheless, it remained 43 members below the orchestra and 115 below the choir at the end.`,
  'telemedicine': `The graph compares remote consultations as a proportion of all specialist consultations in three medical fields from 2015 to 2025. Mental health consistently had the highest share, while physiotherapy had the lowest. All fields increased substantially overall, although their trends included a reversal after 2021.

In 2015, remote appointments accounted for 11% of mental-health consultations, 7% in dermatology and 3% in physiotherapy. These shares rose to 27%, 16% and 9% respectively by 2019. A much larger increase followed, bringing the 2021 figures to 54%, 38% and 24%.

The proportions then declined in all three fields in 2023, to 49% for mental health, 32% for dermatology and 20% for physiotherapy. By 2025 they had recovered to 58%, 35% and 22%, maintaining the same ranking seen throughout the period.

Mental health was the only field to finish above its 2021 level, exceeding it by four percentage points. Dermatology and physiotherapy ended three and two points below their respective peaks. Overall growth was strongest in mental health, whose remote share increased by 47 percentage points from the first observation.`,
  'weekday-swimming': `The graph presents average weekday pool admissions before 9 a.m., during the day and after 5 p.m. between 2016 and 2025. Use increased overall in every period. Daytime admissions started and ended highest, but evening use led briefly, while early-morning swimming grew steadily from the lowest base.

In 2016, the daytime period attracted 240 admissions, compared with 180 in the evening and 95 before 9 a.m. Daytime use rose to 252 in 2018 but then fell to 235 in 2020. Evening admissions increased over the same interval, reaching 228 and almost closing the gap.

By 2022, evening use had overtaken daytime use, at 271 against 260 admissions. It stayed ahead in 2024, with 286 compared with 278. The final year reversed this order: daytime admissions reached 289 while the evening figure slipped to 281.

Morning use rose at every observation, passing 150 admissions in 2022 and ending at 183. Its overall gain of 88 exceeded the daytime increase of 49 but was below the evening gain of 101. Despite that growth, mornings remained the least busy of the three periods.`,
};

export const academicProcessModels: Record<string, string> = {
  'beeswax-candles': `The diagram illustrates the workshop manufacture of beeswax candles. Overall, nine stages transform wax collected from honeycombs into wrapped candles. Wax preparation comes first, followed by moulding and cooling, while trimming and packaging finish the process.

Production begins with the collection of wax from honeycombs. The material is transferred to a heated tank, where it melts. The melted wax is then filtered before it is used to form the candles. These initial operations prepare the wax in liquid form for the pouring stage.

Separately within the sequence, cotton wicks are fixed inside the moulds. Once they are in position, wax is poured into the prepared moulds and allowed to cool. The cooling stage produces solid candles that can then be removed from their moulds, completing the change from liquid material to a shaped product.

The final two stages concern finishing and presentation. First, the wicks are trimmed on the removed candles. The candles are then wrapped ready for sale. The process therefore moves from preparation of the raw wax through the assembly of wax and wick to the finishing of individual candles.`,
  'dried-herbs': `The diagram shows the production of dried culinary herbs for retail sale. Overall, harvested herbs pass through selection and cleaning before drying. The dry material is then separated, cut and packed, with labelling as the final operation.

At the beginning, fresh herbs are harvested. Damaged leaves are removed, leaving the material that will continue through the process. The remaining leaves are rinsed, after which surface water is drained away. This completes the initial preparation before the herbs enter the drying stage.

Warm air is used to dry the leaves. Once this stage is complete, the leaves are separated from their stems. The sequence therefore includes two different forms of selection: damaged leaves are taken out before rinsing, whereas stem separation takes place after drying.

The dried leaves are subsequently cut, producing the material intended for retail packaging. They are placed in jars, which are sealed, and the jars are labelled for sale. These last operations follow the preparation of the dried leaf product. Across the nine stages, the process changes freshly harvested herbs into cleaned, dried and cut leaves contained in sealed, labelled jars.`,
  'aluminium-bottles': `The diagram outlines the recycling of used aluminium drink containers into new containers. Overall, the sequence moves from collection and cleaning to melting and reshaping the metal. It finishes with the new containers filled and distributed.

Used containers are first collected together. Aluminium is separated from other materials, and the selected metal is washed. The clean aluminium is then crushed into compact blocks, changing the collected containers into a form that proceeds to the melting stage.

The blocks are melted, and the resulting metal is cast into slabs. These slabs are subsequently rolled into sheets. Casting and rolling are therefore distinct shaping stages: the first creates larger solid pieces, while the second prepares sheet material for the manufacture of individual containers.

New containers are formed from the aluminium sheets. They are then filled and distributed, completing the nine-stage route from discarded containers to newly supplied products. The preparation stages occur before melting, while the final container shape is produced only after the cast slabs have been rolled. The overall process consequently combines material recovery with several successive changes in the metal's physical form.`,
  'seawater-treatment': `The diagram shows a simplified process for converting seawater into drinking water. Overall, incoming seawater is screened and filtered before desalination. The collected water is then treated further and stored for distribution, forming a nine-stage sequence.

Seawater first enters the system through the intake. It passes through a coarse screen and then a filter that removes suspended particles. These two preliminary stages occur before the high-pressure pump, preparing the water for the main desalination operation.

The pump drives the filtered seawater at high pressure towards the desalination membranes. After passing through them, desalinated water is collected. This collection stage separates the membrane treatment from the later operations applied to the resulting water.

Minerals are added to the collected water, and the treated water is subsequently disinfected. The final stage holds it in storage ready for distribution. Thus, the process does not end immediately after desalination: mineral addition and disinfection intervene before storage. The diagram groups naturally into preparation of the incoming seawater, membrane-based desalination and final treatment of the water that will enter the distribution system.`,
  'oyster-cycle': `The diagram illustrates a simplified oyster life cycle. Overall, it forms a repeating sequence from the release of reproductive cells to adult oysters ready to spawn. Development begins in the water, includes a free-swimming larval phase and continues after attachment to a firm surface.

Adult oysters initially release eggs and sperm. Fertilisation takes place in the water, after which free-swimming larvae develop. The larvae then acquire a small shell while still in the early part of the cycle. Shell development is shown before the change to an attached form.

Next, the larvae attach themselves to a firm surface. The young oysters grow there, linking the attachment stage with later development. They subsequently reach adulthood, completing the transition from larvae to mature oysters.

The adults become ready to spawn, and a return arrow leads from this final stage back to the release of eggs and sperm. Reproduction therefore starts the same sequence again. The principal contrast within the cycle is between the mobile larval stages in the water and the growth stages following attachment, with adult spawning connecting one generation's development to the next.`,
  'cork-flooring': `The diagram shows how harvested cork is made into flooring tiles. Overall, raw bark is dried, cleaned and reduced to granules before being bound and pressed. The formed material is then cut, surface-treated and packed as the finished flooring product.

First, cork bark is harvested and placed in storage to dry. The dried bark is cleaned and then ground into granules. These initial stages prepare the material for mixing, so the binder is added to granulated cork rather than to whole pieces of freshly harvested bark.

The cork granules are mixed with binder, and the resulting mixture is pressed into blocks. Cutting follows pressing: the larger formed blocks are divided into tiles. This provides a clear sequence from small granules, through a larger bound form, to individual flooring units.

A protective surface is subsequently applied to the cut tiles. They are then packed, completing the nine stages. Material preparation is therefore followed by forming and finishing, with the surface treatment occurring after the tile shape has been produced. The final packed tiles are the result of combining and reshaping the cork rather than simply trimming intact sections of bark.`,
  'repair-library': `The diagram explains how a clothing-lending service prepares returned garments for another loan. Overall, the workflow begins with receipt and recording, then moves through inspection, repair and cleaning. A final check and record update precede the garment's return to the lending rail.

When a garment is received, the service first records its return. The fabric and fastenings are then inspected. Staff list the repairs required, and the listed work is completed in the following stage. The repair operations are therefore based on the earlier examination of the garment.

Cleaning takes place after the repairs. The finished garment is then checked, introducing a second inspection point later in the process. The first examination identifies work to be done, whereas this later check follows both repair and cleaning.

Finally, the lending record is updated and the garment is placed on the lending rail. The item becomes available again only after the preparation sequence and its associated record changes. Across the nine stages, handling of the physical garment is combined with administrative recording at the beginning and end, while the central operations restore and check the item for further use.`,
  'rainwater-system': `The diagram illustrates the main collected-water route in a household rainwater-harvesting system. Overall, rainwater moves from the roof through screening and initial diversion into a tank. It is subsequently pumped and filtered before supplying toilets and garden taps.

Rain first falls onto the roof, and gutters gather the resulting runoff. The collected water reaches a leaf screen, where larger debris is removed. It then passes to a first-flush unit, which diverts the initial runoff rather than sending that first portion into storage.

The remaining water enters the storage tank. From there, a pump draws the stored water towards a fine filter. The diagram therefore shows treatment at two separate points: the leaf screen acts before storage, while the fine filter acts on water that has already been stored and pumped.

The final stage supplies the treated water to toilets and garden taps. Collection, storage and reuse are connected in that order, with the tank positioned between the early screening stages and the later pumping and fine filtration. The displayed uses are domestic flushing and garden supply, rather than a general supply to every household outlet.`,
  'glazed-pottery': `The diagram outlines the manufacture of glazed ceramic plant pots. Overall, prepared clay is shaped and dried before two rounds of firing. Glaze is applied between the firings, and inspection and packing follow the second cooling stage.

Production begins with wet clay, which is prepared and shaped into pots on a wheel. The shaped pots are then left to dry. Once dry, they enter the kiln for the first firing, completing the initial sequence of preparation, forming and heating.

The fired pots are cooled before being dipped in liquid glaze. A second firing follows this coating stage. The diagram thus distinguishes the first firing of the dried pot from the later firing of the glazed item, with cooling and glazing occurring between them.

After the second firing, the finished pots are cooled again. They are finally inspected and packed. There are consequently two cooling stages as well as two firings: the first cooling prepares the pots for glazing, while the second precedes the final inspection. The nine-stage sequence moves from wet clay to shaped, fired and glazed pots that are ready to be packed.`,
  'wholegrain-flour': `The diagram shows a simplified process for manufacturing wholegrain flour from wheat. Overall, the grain is cleaned, checked and conditioned before grinding. The ground material is then sieved and its retained fractions blended, with packing completing the sequence.

Harvested wheat is first received at the processing site. Stones and other debris are removed, and the quality of the grain is checked. Water is added to condition the wheat, after which the conditioned grain is allowed to rest. Conditioning and resting therefore occur after the initial cleaning and examination but before milling.

The whole grain is then ground. The resulting material passes through a sieve, and the retained grain fractions are blended. These stages follow the physical breakdown of the wheat and bring the retained components together for the wholegrain product.

Finally, the flour is packed. The nine operations can be divided into preparation of the incoming grain, grinding and handling of the ground flour, and packaging. The central sequence preserves the order of resting, whole-grain grinding, sieving and blending, so the flour reaches the packing stage only after the retained fractions have been combined.`,
};
